0,0,0,389.668,203.239Z\" fill=\"#231f20\"></path><path d=\"M396.994,201.127a8.128,8.128,0,0,1,1.143-4.438,3.549,3.549,0,0,1,6.173-.01,9.235,9.235,0,0,1-.01,8.886,3.522,3.522,0,0,1-6.153-.01A8.081,8.081,0,0,1,396.994,201.127Zm1.706-.02a10.061,10.061,0,0,0,.677,3.925q.679,1.6,1.745,1.6,1.24,0,1.928-1.609a10.238,10.238,0,0,0,.688-4.011,9.566,9.566,0,0,0-.678-3.847q-.678-1.54-1.744-1.541-1.182,0-1.9,1.57A9.4,9.4,0,0,0,398.7,201.107Z\" fill=\"#231f20\"></path></g></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for 2 histograms titled Data Set A and Data Set B\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>Data Set A:\n<ul>\n<li>The horizontal axis is labeled Integer. It ranges from 10 to 60 and is divided into 5 equal intervals.</li>\n<li>The vertical axis is labeled Frequency. It ranges from 0 to 12 in increments of 1, with values marked every 2 grid lines.</li>\n<li>The histogram has a skewed right shape.</li>\n<li>The histogram has 4 bins.</li>\n<li>The Frequency data for the 4 bins are as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>20 to 30: 3</li>\n<li>30 to 40: 4</li>\n<li>40 to 50: 7</li>\n<li>50 to 60: 9</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Data Set B:\n<ul>\n<li>The horizontal axis is labeled Integer. It ranges from 10 to 60 and is divided into 5 equal intervals.</li>\n<li>The vertical axis is labeled Frequency. It ranges from 0 to 12 in increments of 1, with values marked every 2 grid lines.</li>\n<li>The histogram has a skewed right shape.</li>\n<li>The histogram has 4 bins.</li>\n<li>The Frequency data for the 4 bins are as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>10 to 20: 3</li>\n<li>20 to 30: 4</li>\n<li>30 to 40: 7</li>\n<li>40 to 50: 9</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Two data sets of <math alttext=\"23\"><mn>23</mn>\n</math> integers each are summarized in the histograms shown. For each of the histograms, the first interval represents the frequency of integers greater than or equal to <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>, but less than <math alttext=\"20\"><mn>20</mn>\n</math>. The second interval represents the frequency of integers greater than or equal to <math alttext=\"20\"><mn>20</mn>\n</math>, but less than <math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math>, and so on. What is the smallest possible difference between the mean of data set A and the mean of data set B?</p>","externalid":"a4fc06ae-2b85-4830-a27c-e248eebde1f9","templateid":"87ecf682-9416-4a30-82c9-efca4f66a8ef","vaultid":"79dff565-b8ff-470e-af7f-9ae63ab5ceaa","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"2f553685-d466-4242-835c-585de4d41a9c","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"7aed52d2-4f4a-4819-81bf-ef9211cb18a3","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dc3d2a85-87a9-48ef-8c70-6a281d5d8524","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1b5e57f2-9ce4-4c20-bfb4-b2e01b921c65","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"23\"><mn>23</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1691007959833,"updateDate":1691007959833}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959833, 1691007959833),
    ($SATQ$f8f79e11$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$020263-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.F.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inference from sample statistics and margin of error $SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A park ranger asked a random sample of visitors how far they hiked during their visit. Based on the responses, the estimated mean was found to be 4.5 miles, with an associated margin of error of 0.5 miles. Which of the following is the best conclusion from these data?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is likely that all visitors hiked between 4 and 5 miles.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is likely that most visitors hiked exactly 4.5 miles.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is not possible that any visitor hiked less than 3 miles.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is plausible that the mean distance hiked for all visitors is between 4 and 5 miles.</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. The given estimated mean has an associated margin of error because from sample data, the population mean can&rsquo;t be determined precisely. Rather, from the sample mean, an interval can be determined within which it&rsquo;s plausible that the population&rsquo;s mean is likely to lie. Since the estimated mean is 4.5 miles with an associated margin of error of 0.5 miles, it follows that between <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/f8f79e11/img_000_338ac056.png" alt="4 point 5 minus 0 point 5 "></span> miles and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/f8f79e11/img_001_c663f7c2.png" alt="4 point 5 plus 0 point 5"></span> miles, or between 4 and 5 miles, is plausibly the mean distance hiked for all visitors.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Based on the estimated mean, no determination can be made about the number of miles hiked for all visitors to the park.</p></p>
