oint 5 minus 0 point 5 \"></span> miles and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/f8f79e11/img_001_c663f7c2.png\" alt=\"4 point 5 plus 0 point 5\"></span> miles, or between 4 and 5 miles, is plausibly the mean distance hiked for all visitors.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Based on the estimated mean, no determination can be made about the number of miles hiked for all visitors to the park.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":2,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959616,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"f8f79e11","skill_cd":"Q.F.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","createDate":1691007959616,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"020263-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"291ca418-ba54-48c5-8d87-ce47d383a200","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"020263-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A park ranger asked a random sample of visitors how far they hiked during their visit. Based on the responses, the estimated mean was found to be 4.5 miles, with an associated margin of error of 0.5 miles. Which of the following is the best conclusion from these data?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is likely that all visitors hiked between 4 and 5 miles.</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is likely that most visitors hiked exactly 4.5 miles.</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is not possible that any visitor hiked less than 3 miles.</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">It is plausible that the mean distance hiked for all visitors is between 4 and 5 miles.</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The given estimated mean has an associated margin of error because from sample data, the population mean can&rsquo;t be determined precisely. Rather, from the sample mean, an interval can be determined within which it&rsquo;s plausible that the population&rsquo;s mean is likely to lie. Since the estimated mean is 4.5 miles with an associated margin of error of 0.5 miles, it follows that between <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 point 5 minus 0 point 5 \"></span> miles and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 point 5 plus 0 point 5\"></span> miles, or between 4 and 5 miles, is plausibly the mean distance hiked for all visitors.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Based on the estimated mean, no determination can be made about the number of miles hiked for all visitors to the park.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959616,"updateDate":1691007959616}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959616, 1691007959616),
    ($SATQ$fa7a0164$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$09906-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div xmlns="http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1" class="stimulus_reference ">
        <div class="passage ">
          <div class="prose style:1 ">
            <p class="passage_para ">The table below shows the high and low temperatures&nbsp;in Houston, Texas, during a five-day&nbsp;period.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="standalone_image ">
          <img src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/fa7a0164/img_000_ef4c7390.png" alt="The figure presents a 6-column table, with two rows of data, titled &ldquo;Temperatures in Houston, Texas,&rdquo; in degrees Fahrenheit. The first column has no heading. Columns 2 through 6 have the following headings: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday, respectively. The 2 rows of data are as follows. 
Row 1. High temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, for Monday through Friday, respectively: 73, 56, 62, 75, 81.
Row 2. Low temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, for Monday through Friday, respectively: 49, 37, 41, 54, 63.
" width="318" height="80">
        </div>
      </div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">What was the mean low temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, during the five-day period?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">48.8</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">49</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">59</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">59.1</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. The mean low temperature can be calculated by finding the sum of the low temperatures for all the days shown in the table, 49 + 37 + 41 + 54 + 63 = 244, and then dividing the sum by the number of days the temperature was recorded,&nbsp;<span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/fa7a0164/img_001_563bd7a3.png" alt="244 divided by 5, equals 48 point 8"></span>.<p>Choice B is incorrect. This may be the result of choosing the median rather than calculating the mean.&nbsp;Choices C and D are incorrect and may be the result of calculation errors.</p></p>
