 R\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>Line segment upper Q upper R is extended to point upper S.</li>\n<li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper P upper Q upper R\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>P</mi>\n\t<mi>Q</mi>\n\t<mi>R</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>,&nbsp;<math alttext=\"line segment upper Q upper R\"><menclose notation=\"top\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></menclose></math> is extended to point <math alttext=\"upper S\"><mi>S</mi>\n</math>. The measure of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper P upper Q upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"132 degree\"><mn>132</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the measure of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper P upper R upper S\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"163 degree\"><mn>163</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper Q upper P upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi></math>?</p>","keys":["6bd2312a-b7ba-416c-ba48-3b068cbf37b7"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"32702ab7-8dd9-4184-8a4a-113367b71a1c","content":"<p><math alttext=\"48 degree\"><mn>48</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"6bd2312a-b7ba-416c-ba48-3b068cbf37b7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"31 degree\"><mn>31</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"89e8c75f-2309-41e7-ba64-99df770c12aa","content":"<p><math alttext=\"24 degree\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"},{"id":"1c211455-8644-4add-b55c-d81e5ab3ea22","content":"<p><math alttext=\"17 degree\"><mn>17</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. In the figure shown, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper Q upper S\"><mover><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is a line segment, the sum of the measures of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper R upper S\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper R upper Q\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper R upper S\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"163 degree\"><mn>163</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Thus, the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper R upper Q\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus 163 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>163</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"17 degree\"><mn>17</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper Q upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"132 degree\"><mn>132</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Therefore, the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Q upper P upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 180 minus 17 minus 132 right parenthesis degree\"><mo>(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>132</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"31 degree\"><mn>31</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.&nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the measure of the supplement of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper Q upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math>, not the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Q upper P upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect&nbsp;and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper P upper R upper Q\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi></math>, not the measure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper Q upper P upper R\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi></math>.</p>","externalid":"2fc71dbf-343a-44f3-80ee-5b7f22a564da","correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1755117017717,"updateDate":1755117017717}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017717, 1755117017717),
    ($SATQ$02b02213$SATQ$, $SATQ$cde76af2-5837-4a16-aae2-109dd079e384$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d3e9ff44-86d7-4214-a962-6417220d28ad$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">What is the perimeter, in inches, of a rectangle with a length of <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> inches and a width of <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math> inches?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"141cc90c-beca-45f0-9b31-cd6ec31b630f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"b65a5a8d-da8b-4068-871a-f5d73af96aab","content":"<p><math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"558058f7-b5e3-4be0-9267-cee91d503eb5","content":"<p><math alttext=\"22\"><mn>22</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dc221d8c-5c5a-4445-a4a5-2d58bd0428d0","content":"<p><math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dc221d8c-5c5a-4445-a4a5-2d58bd0428d0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is correct. The perimeter of a figure is equal to the sum of the measurements of the sides of the figure. It&rsquo;s given that the rectangle has a length of <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> inches and a width of <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math> inches. Since a rectangle has <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> sides, of which opposite sides are parallel and equal, it follows that the rectangle has two sides with a length of <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> inches and two sides with a width of <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math> inches. Therefore, the perimeter of this rectangle is <math alttext="4 plus 4 plus 9 plus 9"><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn></math>, or <math alttext="26"><mn>26</mn>
</math> inches.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the sum, in inches, of the length and the width of the rectangle.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the sum, in inches, of the two lengths and the width of the rectangle.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the sum, in inches, of the length and the two widths of the rectangle.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"02b02213","external_id":"cde76af2-5837-4a16-aae2-109dd079e384","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"d3e9ff44-86d7-4214-a962-6417220d28ad","uId":"f305513e-c068-453f-a83a-4eb7529501b0","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">What is the perimeter, in inches, of a rectangle with a length of <math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math> inches and a width of <math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math> inches?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"141cc90c-beca-45f0-9b31-cd6ec31b630f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"b65a5a8d-da8b-4068-871a-f5d73af96aab","content":"<p><math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"558058f7-b5e3-4be0-9267-cee91d503eb5","content":"<p><math alttext=\"22\"><mn>22</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dc221d8c-5c5a-4445-a4a5-2d58bd0428d0","content":"<p><math alttext=\"26\"><mn>26</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["dc221d8c-5c5a-4445-a4a5-2d58bd0428d0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is correct. The perimeter of a figure is equal to the sum of the measurements of the sides of the figure. It&rsquo;s given that the rectangle has a length of <math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math> inches and a width of <math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math> inches. Since a rectangle has <math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math> sides, of which opposite sides are parallel and equal, it follows that the rectangle has two sides with a length of <math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math> inches and two sides with a width of <math alttext=\"9\